### Entry Log

An entry log sets out each room’s state at handover: walls, floors, doors, goods, keys, meter reads, and any marks or wear. Add dated photos. At lease end, compare both dates. The log can show whether loss arose on the renter’s watch and can back a proper bond demand.

### Who Holds It

The owner must hold a master copy, photo records, and proof that the renter got a copy. The renter ought to hold a copy. An agent may hold one too, yet the owner ought not rely on an agent alone.

### When a Log Has No Weight

A log may carry no weight at a bond clash when:

- no date or handover proof exists;
- no renter assent or comment appears;
- vague notes, such as “good,” appear;
- no clear photos back up the notes;
- rooms, goods, keys, or meter reads are absent;
- later add-ons lack dates or a clear record; or
- the log was made only once the renter had left.

### Home Log

A home log records each room’s state at lease start: walls, floors, doors, goods, keys, and meter data. Add dated photos. At lease end, use the same log to compare normal wear and any new loss. That can support a fair sum from the renter’s bond.

### Who Keeps One

The owner should keep a main, dated copy, photo record, and proof the renter got a copy. The renter should keep a copy. An agent can also keep one, but the owner ought to have ready access.

### When a Log Can Fail

A log may be of no use at a bond row when:

- no date or handover record exists;
- the renter never saw or agreed to the log;
- loose words, such as “good,” are used;
- no clear, dated photos back up notes;
- rooms, goods, keys, or meter data are left out;
- late add-ons have no date or account; or
- the log was made only after the renter left.

### Start-of-let Home Log

A home log sets out each room’s state at lease start: walls, floors, doors, goods, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos. At lease end, use that log to compare normal wear and any new damage. Such a log can back a fair bond award where loss occurs.

### Who Holds It

The owner ought to hold a master copy, dated photos, and proof that the renter got a copy. The renter ought to hold a copy too. A rental agent may also hold one, but the owner needs ready access.

### When It Has No Weight

A log may carry no weight at a bond row when:

- no date or handover record exists;
- the renter was not shown the log or gave no assent;
- vague terms, such as “good,” are used;
- no clear, dated photos back the notes;
- rooms, goods, keys, or meter reads are absent;
- later add-ons lack a date or account; or
- the log was made only after the renter left.

### Start-of-Let Record

A start-of-let record sets out each room’s state at handover: walls, floors, doors, goods, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos. At end of term, use the record to compare normal wear against new damage. A clear record can support a fair bond award.

### Who Keeps It

The owner should keep a master copy, dated photos, and proof the tenant got a copy. The tenant should keep a copy as well. An agent can keep one, yet the owner must be able to get the full record.

### When It Has No Value

A record may have low or no value at a bond case where:

- no date or handover proof exists;
- the tenant was not sent a copy or had no chance to add notes;
- vague words, such as “good,” appear;
- no clear, dated photos back the notes;
- rooms, goods, keys, or meter reads are absent;
- later changes lack a date or account; or
- the record came after the tenant left.

### Start-of-let record

A start-of-let record logs each room’s state when a new let starts. Set out walls, floors, doors, taps, ovens, keys, and meter reads. Add date-marked photos. At end of term, use the record to show what was present at handover and what has altered. Such a record can help a court or scheme reach a fair award.

### Who holds the record

Keep a master copy, plus all photo logs. Send a full copy to the tenant at handover, and ask for notes or approval. The tenant should also keep a copy. An agent may hold records, yet the owner should also hold a full copy.

### When a record has no value

A court or scheme may reject a record where:

- no date appears;
- no photos back up the notes;
- no proof shows the tenant got a copy;
- the tenant had no chance to add notes;
- vague words, such as “good,” are used;
- key counts, meter reads, or room notes are absent; or
- the record was made after handover or altered later.

### Start-of-let log

A start-of-let log records each room’s state on day one: walls, floors, doors, glass, taps, ovens, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos. At lease end, compare the log and photos against the home’s state. Such a log can show whether a proposed charge has support.

### Who keeps the log

The landlord should keep a master copy. Pass the tenant a full copy at handover, and ask for a dated note to show receipt. An agent can also keep a copy, yet the landlord should not rely on an agent alone.

### When a log may have no value

A log may carry no weight where:

- no date or dated photos appear;
- vague labels, such as “good,” appear;
- no room-by-room notes appear;
- no key count or meter read appears;
- no proof shows the tenant got a copy or could add comments;
- the log was made well after handover; or
- later changes lack a date and clear record.

### Entry log

An entry log sets out each room’s state at handover: walls, floors, doors, glass, taps, oven, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos. At end of a let, use the log and photos to assess any loss or harm beyond normal wear.

### Who keeps a copy

The landlord keeps a master copy. The tenant gets a full copy at handover. An agent may also hold one, yet the landlord should keep one as well. Ask the tenant to date and sign a note that shows receipt.

### When a log has no value

A court may put no weight on a log where:

- no date or dated photos appear;
- notes are vague, such as “good”;
- room-by-room notes are absent;
- key totals or meter reads are absent;
- the tenant has no copy;
- the log came long after handover; or
- later edits lack dates and clear notes.

### Start-of-let record

A start-of-let record logs each room’s state as a let starts: walls, floors, doors, glass, taps, oven, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos.

At end of a let, compare the record and photos. Such a record helps show any harm beyond normal wear.

### Who holds copies

Landlord holds a master copy. Tenant gets a full copy at handover. An agent may hold a copy too. Have tenant add a date and a mark on each copy.

### What makes one weak at court

A court may see weak proof where:

- no date or dated photos;
- vague words, such as “good”;
- no room-by-room account;
- no key count or meter read;
- tenant lacks a copy;
- log made well after handover; or
- later changes lack dates and a clear note.

### Start log

A start log records each room’s state as a let starts: walls, floors, doors, glass, taps, cooker, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos.

At end of a let, use the log and photos to assess any harm above normal wear.

### Who keeps a copy

Landlord keeps a main copy. Tenant gets a full copy at handover. A let agent may keep one as well. At handover, ask both to add a date and a mark.

### When a court may reject a log

A log may lack value where:

- no date or dated photos;
- vague notes, such as “OK”;
- no room-by-room record;
- no key count or meter read;
- tenant has no copy;
- log was made after tenant moves; or
- later updates lack a date and clear note.

Think in two linked bits: a send log and a get log.

At send-off, save:
- a dated booking;
- item ID and destination;
- a photo of the item, its label, and its packed state;
- a hand-off scan;
- a signed hand-off slip;
- a cost slip.

At drop-off, save:
- a dated scan, photo, sign-off;
- name of the individual who took it;
- date, time, and site;
- any text message saying it was got.

If an app is used, download the full activity log soon. Apps can delete old data. Put each job’s items in one digital file, plus a second copy in a safe spot. Use a job ID on all docs. Do not edit the initial copy; add a note if a fix is needed.

A good file shows what was sent, when it left, who had it next, when it got to its end site, and who said it was accepted.

Keep tax, sales, and cost docs at least six tax cycles. Keep signed deals, invoices, payment logs, and send/get logs 7 to 10 tax cycles when a dispute could arise. Local tax, data, and field law may set an added time span, so ask a local tax aid office or legal aid clinic to name exact limits.
